    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Week 17 Self-Assessment\"\nsubtitle: \"MCQ Practice & Credibility\"\n---\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nThis quiz serves two purposes: (1) practice for the MCQ exam next week, and (2) understanding research credibility. Work through all the investigations to prepare for the exam format.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Research Credibility\n\n### Question 1: The Replication Crisis\n\nThe \"replication crisis\" refers to:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Too many studies being published</option><option value='answer'>Many published findings failing to replicate in subsequent studies</option><option value=''>Researchers copying each other's work</option><option value=''>Students replicating experiments in labs</option></select>\n\n### Question 2: Questionable Research Practices\n\nWhich of the following is a questionable research practice (QRP)?\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nopts_qrp <- c(\n  \"Pre-registering your hypothesis before data collection\",\n  answer = \"Running multiple analyses and only reporting the significant ones\",\n  \"Using a large sample size\",\n  \"Reporting effect sizes alongside p-values\"\n)\n```\n:::\n\n\n<div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_JCEPGRTYXG'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JCEPGRTYXG\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Pre-registering your hypothesis before data collection</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JCEPGRTYXG\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>Running multiple analyses and only reporting the significant ones</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JCEPGRTYXG\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Using a large sample size</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_JCEPGRTYXG\" value=\"\"></input> <span>Reporting effect sizes alongside p-values</span></label></div>\n\n\n### Question 3: P-Hacking\n\nP-hacking involves:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Using probability incorrectly</option><option value='answer'>Manipulating analyses to achieve p < .05</option><option value=''>Hacking into statistical software</option><option value=''>Using very small p-values</option></select>\n\nWhich of the following is an example of p-hacking?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Removing outliers until the result becomes significant</option><option value=''>Pre-registering exclusion criteria</option><option value=''>Reporting non-significant results</option><option value=''>Calculating effect sizes</option></select>\n\n### Question 4: HARKing\n\nHARKing stands for:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Having Another Research Knowledge</option><option value='answer'>Hypothesising After Results are Known</option><option value=''>Honest And Reliable Knowledge</option><option value=''>High Achievement Research Kudos</option></select>\n\nWhy is HARKing problematic?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>It takes too long</option><option value='answer'>It presents exploratory findings as if they were predicted, inflating false positive rates</option><option value=''>It makes research too easy</option><option value=''>It uses too many participants</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Improving Research Quality\n\n### Question 5: Pre-Registration\n\nPre-registration means:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Registering participants before the study</option><option value='answer'>Publicly documenting your hypotheses and analysis plan before collecting data</option><option value=''>Registering your publication before peer review</option><option value=''>Recording participant details</option></select>\n\nPre-registration helps prevent:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Data collection errors</option><option value='answer'>HARKing and p-hacking</option><option value=''>Participant dropout</option><option value=''>Ethical violations</option></select>\n\n### Question 6: Open Science Practices\n\nWhich practice improves research transparency?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Sharing data and analysis code publicly</option><option value=''>Only publishing significant results</option><option value=''>Using proprietary software</option><option value=''>Keeping methods secret</option></select>\n\n### Question 7: Sample Size and Power\n\nA larger sample size generally:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Guarantees significant results</option><option value='answer'>Increases statistical power to detect real effects</option><option value=''>Makes studies less credible</option><option value=''>Is always unnecessary</option></select>\n\nStatistical power is the probability of:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Getting p < .05 when there is no effect</option><option value='answer'>Detecting a real effect when it exists</option><option value=''>Making a Type I error</option><option value=''>Rejecting a true alternative hypothesis</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Full MCQ Exam Practice\n\nWork through these investigations as if they were exam questions. Each investigation has 5-6 questions.\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 1\n\nA researcher investigates whether listening to classical music while studying improves memory. 40 participants are randomly assigned to study with classical music or in silence, then take a memory test.\n\n1. **Design**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Between-subjects</option><option value=''>Within-subjects</option><option value=''>Matched pairs</option><option value=''>Correlational</option></select>\n\n2. **IV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Study condition (music vs silence)</option><option value=''>Memory test score</option><option value=''>Type of music</option><option value=''>No IV</option></select>\n\n3. **DV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Music condition</option><option value='answer'>Memory test score</option><option value=''>Study duration</option><option value=''>Participant preference</option></select>\n\n4. **Appropriate test**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Independent t-test</option><option value=''>Paired t-test</option><option value=''>Pearson's r</option><option value=''>Chi-square</option></select>\n\n5. **One-tailed hypothesis**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Memory will differ between conditions</option><option value='answer'>Participants studying with music will have higher memory scores than those in silence</option><option value=''>There will be no difference in memory</option><option value=''>Music and memory are correlated</option></select>\n\n6. **Results: t(38) = 1.85, p = .072. The result is**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Significant at α = .05</option><option value='answer'>Not significant at α = .05</option><option value=''>Highly significant</option><option value=''>Cannot determine</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 2\n\nA researcher tests whether exercise affects mood. 25 participants rate their mood before and after a 30-minute exercise session.\n\n1. **Design**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Between-subjects</option><option value='answer'>Within-subjects</option><option value=''>Matched pairs</option><option value=''>Correlational</option></select>\n\n2. **IV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Time (before vs after exercise)</option><option value=''>Mood rating</option><option value=''>Exercise duration</option><option value=''>No IV</option></select>\n\n3. **DV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Time point</option><option value='answer'>Mood rating</option><option value=''>Type of exercise</option><option value=''>Participant fitness</option></select>\n\n4. **Appropriate test**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Independent t-test</option><option value='answer'>Paired t-test</option><option value=''>Pearson's r</option><option value=''>Mann-Whitney U</option></select>\n\n5. **Results: t(24) = 4.23, p < .001, d = 0.85. This is**:\n   - Significant? <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>TRUE</option><option value=''>FALSE</option></select>\n   - Effect size: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Small</option><option value=''>Medium</option><option value='answer'>Large</option><option value=''>Very small</option></select>\n\n6. **Conclusion**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Exercise has no effect on mood</option><option value='answer'>Mood significantly improved after exercise</option><option value=''>Mood significantly decreased after exercise</option><option value=''>The study is flawed</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 3\n\nA researcher examines whether there is a relationship between screen time (hours per day) and sleep quality (score out of 100) in 80 university students.\n\n1. **Design**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Between-subjects</option><option value=''>Within-subjects</option><option value=''>Matched pairs</option><option value='answer'>Correlational</option></select>\n\n2. **IV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Screen time</option><option value=''>Sleep quality</option><option value=''>Age</option><option value='answer'>No IV</option></select>\n\n3. **Variables measured**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Only screen time</option><option value=''>Only sleep quality</option><option value='answer'>Both screen time and sleep quality</option><option value=''>Neither</option></select>\n\n4. **Appropriate test**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Independent t-test</option><option value=''>Paired t-test</option><option value='answer'>Pearson's r</option><option value=''>Wilcoxon test</option></select>\n\n5. **Results: r(78) = -.38, p < .001. This indicates**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>No relationship</option><option value=''>Strong positive relationship</option><option value='answer'>Moderate negative relationship</option><option value=''>Perfect correlation</option></select>\n\n6. **Can we conclude screen time CAUSES poor sleep?** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>TRUE</option><option value='answer'>FALSE</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 4\n\nA researcher investigates whether room temperature affects test performance. One classroom is set to 18°C, another to 24°C. Different students in each room take the same test.\n\n1. **Design**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Between-subjects</option><option value=''>Within-subjects</option><option value=''>Matched pairs</option><option value=''>Correlational</option></select>\n\n2. **IV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Room temperature (18°C vs 24°C)</option><option value=''>Test score</option><option value=''>Classroom</option><option value=''>No IV</option></select>\n\n3. **DV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Temperature</option><option value='answer'>Test score</option><option value=''>Room assignment</option><option value=''>Student comfort</option></select>\n\n4. **Appropriate test**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Independent t-test</option><option value=''>Paired t-test</option><option value=''>Pearson's r</option><option value=''>Chi-square</option></select>\n\n5. **Results: t(58) = 2.67, p = .010, d = 0.69**:\n   - The p-value indicates: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Significant difference at α = .05</option><option value=''>No significant difference</option><option value=''>The effect is large</option><option value=''>The test failed</option></select>\n   - The effect size is: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Small</option><option value='answer'>Medium</option><option value=''>Large</option><option value=''>Negligible</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 5\n\nA researcher pairs participants based on IQ scores, then randomly assigns one member of each pair to a cognitive training program while the other receives no training. Cognitive performance is measured after 6 weeks.\n\n1. **Design**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Between-subjects</option><option value=''>Within-subjects</option><option value='answer'>Matched pairs</option><option value=''>Correlational</option></select>\n\n2. **Why matched pairs?**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>To save time</option><option value='answer'>To control for individual differences in IQ</option><option value=''>To increase sample size</option><option value=''>It's required for all studies</option></select>\n\n3. **IV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Training condition (training vs no training)</option><option value=''>IQ score</option><option value=''>Cognitive performance</option><option value=''>No IV</option></select>\n\n4. **DV**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Training received</option><option value='answer'>Cognitive performance</option><option value=''>IQ match</option><option value=''>Pair membership</option></select>\n\n5. **The appropriate test would be similar to**: <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Independent t-test</option><option value='answer'>Paired t-test (because pairs are not independent)</option><option value=''>Pearson's r</option><option value=''>Chi-square</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Credibility Scenarios\n\n### Question 8: Evaluating Research\n\nA researcher reports: \"After extensive analysis of our data, we discovered an unexpected finding that participants who ate breakfast performed better.\"\n\nWhat credibility concern does this raise?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>The finding is definitely wrong</option><option value='answer'>Possible HARKing — the finding may be presented as predicted when it was exploratory</option><option value=''>The sample was too large</option><option value=''>The researcher used the wrong test</option></select>\n\n### Question 9: Red Flags\n\nWhich of these is a red flag for research credibility?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Large sample size</option><option value=''>Pre-registered hypotheses</option><option value='answer'>Only significant results reported from a study with many variables</option><option value=''>Effect sizes reported</option></select>\n\n### Question 10: Good Practice\n\nWhich researcher is following best practices?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Researcher A who removes outliers until getting p < .05</option><option value=''>Researcher B who only reports their significant findings</option><option value='answer'>Researcher C who pre-registered their hypothesis and reports all results</option><option value=''>Researcher D who changes their hypothesis after seeing the data</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Summary Checklist\n\nBefore the MCQ exam, make sure you can:\n\n- [ ] Identify design type from a research description\n- [ ] Identify IV and DV (or recognise when there's no IV)\n- [ ] Select the appropriate statistical test\n- [ ] Interpret p-values and effect sizes\n- [ ] Distinguish significant from non-significant results\n- [ ] Explain what the replication crisis is\n- [ ] Identify questionable research practices (p-hacking, HARKing)\n- [ ] Describe practices that improve research credibility\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n## MCQ Exam Next Week\nThe MCQ exam is during Wednesday's lecture slot (Week 18). Review all quizzes from Weeks 11-17. The exam follows the investigation format practiced above.\n:::\n",
